\title{Unit-Affine Rerandomization for Learning with Errors\\over Arbitrary Moduli}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{25 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Search-to-decision reductions for Learning with Errors are most transparent over a prime modulus, where every nonzero residue is invertible. Composite moduli, including powers of two, contain nonzero zero divisors, so the prime-field rule for affine rerandomization does not survive unchanged. We isolate the exact arbitrary-modulus replacement and develop its consequences. For every positive modulus $q$, the affine map $x\mapsto ax+b$ on $\Z/q\Z$ is bijective if and only if $a$ is a unit, equivalently if and only if $\gcd(a,q)=1$. Unit-affine maps preserve every finite sum and average, hence preserve the uniform distribution. The admissible multipliers number exactly Euler's totient $\varphi(q)$. Under a Chinese-remainder decomposition into coprime factors, both invertibility and the totient count factor componentwise. Finally, a finite hybrid lemma shows that if an aggregate distinguishing advantage $\delta$ is distributed among $q$ candidate residues of a secret coordinate, then one candidate contributes at least $\delta/q$. These results supply the algebraic and quantitative scaffold required by arbitrary-modulus search-to-decision arguments, recover the prime-modulus theory as a corollary, and identify the distributional steps still required for a complete oracle reduction.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The Learning with Errors problem (LWE) hides a vector behind noisy modular linear equations. Let $q\geq1$ be an integer modulus, $n\geq1$ a dimension, and
\[
\Rq=\Z/q\Z.
\]
A secret $s\in\Rq^n$ determines samples
\[
(u,v)\in\Rq^n\times\Rq,
\qquad v=\langle u,s\rangle+e\pmod q,
\]
where $u$ is uniform and $e$ follows a prescribed distribution concentrated near zero. Search-LWE asks for $s$; Decision-LWE asks whether samples have this form or are uniformly random. A search-to-decision reduction uses a decision oracle repeatedly to recover the secret, commonly by guessing secret coordinates and transforming samples so that the oracle detects a correct guess.

For a prime modulus $p$, the ring $R_p$ is a field. Every nonzero $a$ is invertible, so $x\mapsto ax+b$ permutes $R_p$. This permutation property is the actual reason that affine rerandomization preserves a uniform modular coordinate. For composite $q$, however, nonzero elements can be zero divisors. Modulo $4$, multiplication by $2$ sends $0,2$ to $0$ and $1,3$ to $2$. Translation cannot restore the lost information.

The appropriate replacement for ``nonzero'' is therefore ``unit.'' This paper organizes arbitrary-modulus rerandomization around three facts: units are exactly the valid affine multipliers; their number is $\varphi(q)$; and both units and their count split across coprime Chinese-remainder components. A generic averaging result then supplies the numerical hybrid step: a total advantage spread over $q$ residues leaves at least $\delta/q$ somewhere.

These statements establish a universal algebraic scaffold. A complete probabilistic polynomial-time reduction for a particular LWE formulation must additionally define its oracle experiments, prove compatibility of transformed error distributions, and account for samples and repetitions. Those distributional details depend on the selected LWE parameters and are not implied by finite-ring algebra alone.

\section{Preliminaries}

\begin{definition}[Unit]
A residue $a\in\Rq$ is a \emph{unit} if some $c\in\Rq$ satisfies $ac=1$. The units form the multiplicative group $\units{\Rq}$.
\end{definition}

For an integer representative $a$, B\'ezout's identity gives
\[
a\in\units{\Rq}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\gcd(a,q)=1.
\]
Indeed, $ua+vq=1$ makes $u$ an inverse modulo $q$; a modular inverse conversely yields such an integer relation.

\begin{definition}[Affine transformation]
For $a,b\in\Rq$, define
\[
T_{a,b}:\Rq\to\Rq,\qquad T_{a,b}(x)=ax+b.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Euler's totient]
Euler's totient is
\[
\varphi(q)=\bigl|\{a\in\{0,\ldots,q-1\}:\gcd(a,q)=1\}\bigr|.
\]
Thus $\varphi(q)$ counts the units of $\Rq$.
\end{definition}

If $X$ is uniform on $\Rq$ and $f:\Rq\to\R$, then
\[
\E[f(X)]=\frac1q\sum_{x\in\Rq}f(x).
\]
Permutations preserve this average for every $f$, and therefore preserve the full uniform distribution.

A finite hybrid assigns real contributions $A(i)$ to indices in a nonempty finite set $I$. We require only a lower bound
\[
\delta\leq\sum_{i\in I}A(i).
\]
No positivity assumption on the individual terms is needed for the averaging result below.

\section{The affine permutation criterion}

\begin{theorem}[Affine Permutation Criterion]\label{thm:affine}
Let $q>0$ and $a,b\in\Rq$. The affine map $T_{a,b}(x)=ax+b$ is bijective if and only if $a$ is a unit of $\Rq$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Translation by $b$ is bijective, with inverse translation by $-b$. Thus $T_{a,b}$ is bijective exactly when multiplication $M_a(x)=ax$ is bijective. If $a$ is a unit, multiplication by $a^{-1}$ is the inverse of $M_a$. Conversely, if $M_a$ is onto, there is an $x$ with $ax=1$, so $a$ is a unit. If one starts with bijectivity of $T_{a,b}$, composing with translation by $-b$ gives bijectivity of $M_a$.
\end{proof}

When $a$ is a unit the inverse is explicit:
\[
T_{a,b}^{-1}(y)=a^{-1}(y-b).
\]
No field structure is required; only the selected multiplier must be invertible.

\begin{corollary}[Coprimality Criterion]\label{cor:coprime}
For an integer $a$ and $b\in\Rq$, the map $x\mapsto ax+b$ is bijective if and only if $\gcd(a,q)=1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:affine} with the B\'ezout characterization of invertibility modulo $q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
For $q=12$, the multipliers $1,5,7,11$ are units and every corresponding affine map permutes all twelve residues. The nonzero multiplier $4$ is invalid: multiplication by $4$ has image only $\{0,4,8\}$.
\end{example}

\section{Invariance of sums and uniformity}

\begin{theorem}[Unit-Affine Sum Invariance]\label{thm:sum}
Let $q>0$, let $a,b\in\Rq$, and suppose $a$ is a unit. For every $f:\Rq\to\R$,
\[
\sum_{x\in\Rq}f(ax+b)=\sum_{x\in\Rq}f(x).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:affine} makes $x\mapsto ax+b$ a permutation. Reindex the sum by $y=ax+b$; every residue occurs exactly once.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Average Invariance]
Under the same hypotheses,
\[
\frac1q\sum_{x\in\Rq}f(ax+b)=\frac1q\sum_{x\in\Rq}f(x).
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Divide Theorem~\ref{thm:sum} by $q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Preservation of Uniformity]\label{cor:uniform}
If $X$ is uniform on $\Rq$ and $a$ is a unit, then $aX+b$ is uniform on $\Rq$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:sum} to indicator functions. Equivalently, each $y$ has the unique preimage $a^{-1}(y-b)$, so $\Pr[aX+b=y]=1/q$.
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform} is the universal uniformity engine used by an LWE hybrid. A decision oracle is distribution-sensitive, so a transformation before an oracle call must not manufacture a bias. The theorem covers every decision statistic at once. A concrete reduction still needs a separate argument connecting correct and incorrect secret guesses to its particular oracle distributions and error law.

\section{Counting admissible rerandomizers}

\begin{theorem}[Totient Enumeration]\label{thm:totient}
For every $q>0$, exactly $\varphi(q)$ residues $a\in\Rq$ make $x\mapsto ax+b$ bijective. The count is independent of $b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:affine}, admissible multipliers are precisely the units. By Corollary~\ref{cor:coprime}, these are precisely the classes represented by integers coprime to $q$, whose defining count is $\varphi(q)$.
\end{proof}

Since $1$ is always a unit and units are residues,
\[
0<\varphi(q)\leq q.
\]
Thus the valid set is never empty.

\begin{corollary}[Prime Modulus Count]
If $p$ is prime, then exactly $p-1$ affine multipliers are valid.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Every nonzero residue modulo $p$ is coprime to $p$, so $\varphi(p)=p-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Rejection-Sampling Cost]\label{prop:sampling}
Suppose independent uniform residues modulo $q$ are drawn until a unit appears. The success probability per trial is $\varphi(q)/q$, and the expected number of trials is $q/\varphi(q)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:totient} gives the success probability. The waiting time is geometric and has mean equal to the reciprocal success probability.
\end{proof}

For a prime power,
\[
\varphi(p^e)=p^e-p^{e-1}=p^e\left(1-\frac1p\right).
\]
The unit density is therefore $1-1/p$, independent of $e$. Fixed-prime powers have constant expected rejection cost; powers of two require two proposals on average.

\section{Chinese-remainder decomposition}

If $m$ and $n$ are coprime, the Chinese Remainder Theorem supplies a ring isomorphism
\[
\Psi:R_{mn}\longrightarrow R_m\times R_n,
\qquad \Psi(a)=(a\bmod m,a\bmod n).
\]

\begin{theorem}[Componentwise Invertibility]\label{thm:crtunit}
If $\gcd(m,n)=1$, then $a\in R_{mn}$ is a unit if and only if both components of $\Psi(a)$ are units in $R_m$ and $R_n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A pair $(u,v)$ in a product ring is invertible exactly when both entries are invertible. Component inverses combine as $(u^{-1},v^{-1})$, while an inverse of the pair supplies an inverse in each coordinate. Transport this statement through $\Psi$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicativity of the Rerandomizer Count]\label{thm:mult}
If $\gcd(m,n)=1$, then
\[
\varphi(mn)=\varphi(m)\varphi(n).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:crtunit} identifies every unit modulo $mn$ with an arbitrary pair of units modulo $m$ and $n$. Count this Cartesian product.
\end{proof}

For $q=\prod_{j=1}^r p_j^{e_j}$, iteration gives
\[
\units{R_q}\cong\prod_{j=1}^r\units{R_{p_j^{e_j}}},
\qquad
\varphi(q)=\prod_{j=1}^r\varphi(p_j^{e_j})
=q\prod_{p\mid q}\left(1-\frac1p\right).
\]
A multiplier is globally safe exactly when it is safe in every prime-power component.

\begin{example}
For $q=40=5\cdot8$, Theorem~\ref{thm:mult} gives
\[
\varphi(40)=\varphi(5)\varphi(8)=4\cdot4=16.
\]
A residue is a unit modulo $40$ precisely when it is nonzero modulo $5$ and odd modulo $8$.
\end{example}

\section{The finite hybrid bound}

\begin{theorem}[Advantage Pigeonhole Principle]\label{thm:pigeon}
Let $I$ be a finite nonempty set, $A:I\to\R$, and $\delta\in\R$. If
\[
\delta\leq\sum_{i\in I}A(i),
\]
then some $i\in I$ satisfies
\[
A(i)\geq\frac{\delta}{|I|}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Otherwise $A(i)<\delta/|I|$ for every $i$. Strict summation over the nonempty set yields
\[
\sum_{i\in I}A(i)<\sum_{i\in I}\frac{\delta}{|I|}=\delta,
\]
contradicting the hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Residue-Wise Advantage Bound]\label{cor:residue}
Let $q>0$ and assign $A(r)\in\R$ to every $r\in\Rq$. If
\[
\delta\leq\sum_{r\in\Rq}A(r),
\]
then some residue satisfies $A(r)\geq\delta/q$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:pigeon} to the $q$-element set $\Rq$.
\end{proof}

The denominator $q$ counts possible secret residues; the quantity $\varphi(q)$ counts safe multipliers. These are different roles and must not be conflated. Once a concrete hybrid associates one contribution with each candidate secret residue and proves the required sum bound, Corollary~\ref{cor:residue} guarantees a candidate with at least a $1/q$ share of the total gap. It does not, without additional algorithmic structure, identify that candidate.

The same theorem over $n$ coordinate indices yields a contribution of at least $\delta/n$. A nested coordinate-and-residue hybrid may incur a loss on the scale of $nq$, depending on its exact organization and sign conventions.

\section{Recovery of the prime case}

\begin{corollary}[Prime-Field Affine Bijection]
Let $p$ be prime. If $a,b\in R_p$ and $a\neq0$, then $x\mapsto ax+b$ is a bijection.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Every nonzero element of a field is a unit, so Theorem~\ref{thm:affine} applies.
\end{proof}

Thus arbitrary-modulus theory contains the prime theory rather than replacing it. The field assumption merely allowed ``nonzero'' to serve as shorthand for the actual invariant, ``invertible.''

\section{Algorithms and computational implications}

To validate and apply an affine transformation, compute $g=\gcd(a,q)$. Reject when $g\neq1$; otherwise return $ax+b\pmod q$. The Euclidean algorithm runs in time polynomial in $\log q$. The extended Euclidean algorithm also produces $a^{-1}$ and hence the inverse transformation.

A uniform unit can be sampled by rejection: repeatedly choose a uniform residue and retain it exactly when its gcd with $q$ is $1$. Conditional on acceptance, uniformity among units is immediate because all proposals had equal probability. Proposition~\ref{prop:sampling} gives the exact expected proposal count. If the factorization of $q$ is available, units may instead be sampled independently in prime-power components and recombined by the Chinese Remainder Theorem.

Sum invariance admits a direct numerical demonstration. For values $f(0),\ldots,f(q-1)$ compute
\[
S=\sum_{x=0}^{q-1}f(x),
\qquad
S_{a,b}=\sum_{x=0}^{q-1}f((ax+b)\bmod q).
\]
A unit multiplier guarantees $S=S_{a,b}$. A nonunit can repeat outputs, so equality is not generally expected. This provides a transparent diagnostic for the information loss caused by zero divisors.

\section{Applications to LWE reductions}

The results play three separate roles. First, Theorem~\ref{thm:affine} specifies legal rerandomizers and prevents collisions from creating an artificial decision signal. Second, Theorem~\ref{thm:sum} establishes distribution preservation for every statistic, so uniformity need not be reproved for each distinguisher. Third, Corollary~\ref{cor:residue} quantifies a local signal after the endpoint gap has been decomposed among candidate residues.

The Chinese-remainder theorems suggest a modular reduction architecture. Factor the ring into prime-power components, analyze sample and error marginals, and replace components along a hybrid chain. Unit algebra already commutes with this decomposition. The remaining work is probabilistic: the LWE distribution and its error law must have the compatibility needed by the component experiments.

These statements do not assert an unconditional equivalence of every possible Search-LWE and Decision-LWE definition. Hardness and reductions depend on parameter regimes, error distributions, secret distributions, sample access, and oracle models. The results remove the universal affine obstruction and make the finite advantage bookkeeping exact; concrete reductions must instantiate the rest.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The conceptual shift is from field language to ring language. Composite rings reveal that invertibility, not nonzeroness, was the property doing the work. The totient then separates correctness from cost: correctness restricts multipliers to units, while $\varphi(q)/q$ measures how frequently a random proposal is valid. This ratio remains constant for powers of a fixed prime and decreases for integers containing many distinct small prime factors.

A first research direction is a full distributional Chinese-remainder hybrid. For
\[
q=\prod_{j=1}^r p_j^{e_j},
\]
replace components one at a time. An endpoint gap $\delta$ should force an adjacent component replacement with gap at least $\delta/r$, provided the error distribution admits the corresponding marginals.

A second direction is digitwise recovery for $q=p^e$. The chain
\[
(p^{e-1})\subset\cdots\subset(p)\subset R_{p^e}
\]
has successive quotients isomorphic to $R_p$. One may therefore seek to expose one base-$p$ digit of a secret coordinate per layer while retaining unit-affine rerandomization in the ambient ring. Precise error stability under reduction modulo $p^k$ is required.

A third direction is sharp rerandomization complexity. Since
\[
\frac{\varphi(q)}q=\prod_{p\mid q}\left(1-\frac1p\right),
\]
distinct small prime factors uniquely drive the density downward. Classical asymptotics suggest a worst-family unit density on the scale of $1/\log\log q$ and rejection cost on the scale of $\log\log q$, whereas fixed-prime powers retain constant expected cost. Integrating such estimates into end-to-end oracle complexity would expose the quantitative role of modulus selection.

\section{Conclusion}

Affine rerandomization over $R_q$ is lossless exactly when its multiplier is a unit. This criterion is equivalent to coprimality with $q$, preserves every sum and average, admits exactly $\varphi(q)$ choices, and factors over coprime Chinese-remainder components. A finite hybrid guarantees a contribution of at least $\delta/q$ among $q$ candidate residues whenever their total is at least $\delta$. The prime case follows because nonzero residues and units coincide in a field.

Together these results give the correct algebraic and quantitative language for arbitrary-modulus LWE search-to-decision analysis. They explain why the naive prime-field substitution fails, how to repair it, how much the repair costs, and how the repaired structure decomposes. The remaining challenge is to integrate this universal scaffold with concrete LWE error distributions and oracle experiments.

